ardware is  hardware uh those are just tools and you  know what the um a lot these are  fledgling forces even though it seems  like i mean 20 years is a long time  we invested most of our youth our entire  adult life uh fighting over there and  working over there uh with these folks  and but what i can just tell you is that  you know this insurgent is an extremely  adept insurgent and and very organized  and have been playing applying  consistent pressure against coalition  forces and certainly the afghan forces  for many many years um and it takes time  it takes time to build that kind of  capacity and they needed more time  certainly with our advisors at their  side and i hope that's a lesson that the  whole the western community that nato  learns is that when we go in capacity  building is a real thing but we when you  break it you buy it you've got to stay  in there and and work shoulder to  shoulder for the duration and and it  doesn't have to be a hundred thousand  troops but we do need to stay with them  during these rough times but 20 years is  a long time um i was speaking to someone  who who advised president trump about  the withdrawal and he said we couldn't  stay there forever  well you know what i would point to is  there you know the united states uh  great britain they have they have a long  history of capacity building and and  working surgically in countries that are  at risk and helping them build up you  know an antibody to uh extremism which  is a local populace or a partner force  that's willing to stand their ground i  mean i point to the philippines to  colombia certainly for the united states  where we've had immense success in doing  that and and i think it's something that  as we face violent extremists like isis  and al-qaeda in the future we're going  to need the ability to build capacity in  these countries and it's a multi-decade  endeavor but we can't do it with these  heavy footprints like we did in  afghanistan it's got to be a more